# trinoris-secure — read before touching any source file

This repo (`@trinoris/securegit`'s own repository) dogfoods itself: outside
`docs/`, `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `.github/workflows/`, `.github/actions/`,
`.claude/`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursor/`,
`.windsurfrules`, `.clinerules` (this file), package manifests, and
`secret-pass-phrase.txt`/`recovery-code.txt`/`*.recovery.txt` themselves,
every file is real securegit ciphertext at rest. A file whose content
is garbled binary starting with `SECUREGIT` is not corrupted — decrypt
the checkout before treating it as broken.

## Unlock first

```sh
securegit --version 2>&1 || npm install -g @trinoris/securegit
securegit install
SECUREGIT_RECOVERY_CODE="$(tail -1 recovery-code.txt)" SECUREGIT_PASSPHRASE="$(tail -1 secret-pass-phrase.txt)" \
  securegit key import-recovery --in trinoris-secure.recovery.txt
SECUREGIT_PASSPHRASE="$(tail -1 secret-pass-phrase.txt)" securegit unlock
git rm --cached -r -q . && git checkout HEAD -- .
```

Two real mistakes silently break this, both confirmed on this exact
repo:

- Use `tail -1`, never `cat`, on `secret-pass-phrase.txt` and
  `recovery-code.txt` — both files are mostly explanatory prose; `cat`
  reads the whole file as the secret and fails `unlock` with "wrong
  passphrase" every time, silently.
- Never skip the `import-recovery` line. `securegit unlock` alone only
  works on the one machine that originally ran `securegit init` — it
  decrypts a *local* keyring (`~/.securegit/repos/<repoId>/keyring.json`)
  never committed to Git by design. Any other machine fails with "no
  keyring found for this repository" regardless of how correct the
  passphrase is; `import-recovery` rebuilds that keyring from the
  committed recovery file and is safe to run even when a keyring
  already exists.

The final `git rm --cached`/`checkout` line is required — Git's
stat-cache skips re-running `smudge` on an already-ciphertext file even
right after `unlock` without it.

## Verify, don't assume

Every command above can exit 0 while still locked:

```sh
head -c 20 packages/securegit/src/cli.ts   # expect a real // comment
securegit verify --json                    # every "ok" must be true
```

The passphrase is published on purpose — this is the open-source
security tool itself, so hiding its own source would defeat the point
of being open source. Full reasoning: README.md's "This repository
dogfoods itself".
